{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TTzkbZFiaU8Xv6nBo18wDi","type":"tool_result","content":"/bin/bash: /etc/bash.bashrc: Permission denied\nmetais unpack — Write a packed package's source directory, to edit it.\n\nUsage:\n  metais unpack <package> --to <dir> [--dry-run] [--limit <name>=<value>]...\n                [--json]\n\nWrites every entry of the packed package into <dir>, as the source directory it\nwas packed from: the manifest as its author wrote it, the inputs, templates,\nreferences, assets and examples. .compiled/ is not written: it is derived from\nthe rest, and metais pack writes it again; nor is README.md, the specification's\ntext, which pack also writes. Files are written with mode 0644 and directories\n0755, less the umask, whatever the package records.\n\nBefore it writes anything, unpack verifies the whole package as inspect does,\nits digests included, and refuses a package that fails, writing nothing. It does\nnot run check: a package with check errors is the one an author needs to unpack\nand fix. A source directory is refused: it is already what unpack would write.\n\n--to is required. A <dir> that does not exist is created, with its missing\nparents, by writing beside it and renaming the whole directory into place, so a\nfailed write leaves nothing. An existing <dir> must be empty, apart from names\nbeginning with . (such as .git) other than .compiled, and files ending .metais;\nit is written in place, and a failed write stops unpack, which lists the files\nit wrote and removes none of them. There is no --overwrite: a file left from an\nearlier tree would join the unpacked source and be packed with it.\n\n--dry-run lists the files unpack would write, and writes nothing anywhere. It is\nrefused exactly when unpack would be refused before writing, with the same\nfindings: a package that fails to open, a source directory, a <dir> that is not\nempty or not a directory, and a staging directory left beside a <dir> that does\nnot exist. What only writing finds out, such as a full disk, it cannot report.\n\nOperands:\n  <package>\n      A packed package file; - is refused, since a package is read with random\n      access. Required.\n\nFlags:\n  --to <dir>\n      The directory to write the source directory into: one that does not exist\n      yet, or an empty one. Required.\n  --dry-run\n      List the files unpack would write, and write nothing.\n  --limit <name>=<value>\n      Lower one resource limit for this run, to a whole number no higher than\n      its default (a size may end in KiB, MiB or GiB). metais limits lists them.\n      May be given more than once.\n  --fail-on <severity>\n      With warning, a warning fails the command with exit status 1 as an error\n      does; no finding's severity changes. Values: error, warning. Default:\n      error.\n  --json\n      Print the result as one JSON document on standard output.\n  --color <when>\n      Colour human output: auto colours a stream that is a terminal when\n      NO_COLOR is unset or empty; always and never override NO_COLOR. Values:\n      auto, always, never. Default: auto.\n  -h, --help\n      Print help to standard output and exit 0; nothing else runs.\n\nPrints:\n  The files written, or with --dry-run that would be, relative to <dir>, in the\n  order written. With --json, one metais.unpack/1 document.\n\nRefusals:\n  limit.invalid\n      A --limit names no limit, gives a malformed value, or tries to raise one.\n      Next: metais limits\n  limit.package\n      The package breaches one of its limits.\n      Next: metais limits\n  output.destination.not_directory\n      <dir>, or a directory above it, exists and is not a directory.\n      Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  output.destination.not_empty\n      <dir> holds an entry other than the quiet ones.\n      Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  output.destination.nothing_written\n      A write failed and nothing was left: into a new directory, at any point;\n      into an existing one, before it created anything.\n      Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  output.destination.write_failed\n      A write into an existing directory failed after it had created something\n      there; the files already written are listed and left in place.\n      Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  usage.bad_value\n      A flag's value is missing, or is not one the flag accepts, or a flag that\n      takes no value is given one.\n      Next: metais unpack --help\n  usage.missing_operand\n      The package operand is not given.\n      Next: metais unpack --help\n  usage.not_a_packed_package\n      The package operand is a source directory, which is already what unpack\n      writes.\n      Next: metais inspect <package>\n  usage.package_from_stdin\n      The package operand is -, standard input.\n      Next: metais unpack --help\n  usage.repeated_flag\n      A flag that may be given once is given again.\n      Next: metais unpack --help\n  usage.unexpected_operand\n      An operand the command does not take.\n      Next: metais unpack --help\n  usage.unknown_flag\n      An argument that is not a flag the command accepts, an abbreviated flag,\n      or one-letter aliases written together.\n      Next: metais unpack --help\n\n  Each line below is a family of codes: how many there are, the cause they share\n  and their next step. 'metais help refusals unpack' lists every code, and help\n  --json gives each code's own cause.\n\n  manifest.* (44)  The manifest breaks one of its rules.\n                   Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  package.* (47)   The package is refused.\n                   Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  schema.* (10)    An input schema is refused.\n                   Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n  template.* (9)   An output's path, when or for_each does not parse.\n                   Next: metais unpack <package> --to <dir>\n\nExamples:\n  metais unpack report-1.0.0.metais --to report\n      Writes the package's source directory into the new directory report.\n  metais unpack report-1.0.0.metais --to . --json\n      Writes it into the current directory, which must be empty, and lists the\n      files as one metais.unpack/1 document.\n  metais unpack report-1.0.0.metais --to report --dry-run\n      Lists the files it would write into report, and writes nothing.\n----\nmetais pack — Check and test a source directory, then write it as one package\nfile.\n\nUsage:\n  metais pack [<dir>] [--out <file>|-] [--dry-run] [--limit <name>=<value>]...\n              [--json]\n\nRuns metais check on the source directory, the current directory when omitted.\nAny error stops pack: every finding is reported and nothing is written. With no\nerror it runs metais test over every example; an example that fails stops it the\nsame way. Warnings are reported and do not stop it. There is no way to skip a\nstep: the bytes a supplier receives are the bytes whose examples passed.\n\nThe package begins with README.md, stored: the specification's text, which says\nwhat the file is and where to find metais. It then holds metais.yaml and every\nfile of inputs/, templates/, references/, assets/ and examples/, names beginning\nwith . included, and .compiled/, computed afresh. A top-level name beginning\nwith ., a top-level README.md, and a top-level file ending .metais, are left out\nsilently; any other top-level name is left out and reported as\npackage.layout.not_packed. Each file is packed byte for byte. The package's\nbytes depend only on its entries' paths and contents: never on a file's time,\nmode or owner, the order a directory lists its names in, the platform, the\nlocale or the clock. One build of metais packs a source directory to the same\nbytes every time. The package is read back as every reader reads one before it\nis kept, and a limit only its bytes show, such as entry-ratio, refuses it as\nlimit.package. An entry whose bytes put the end of central directory signature\nin the package's final 65,557 bytes refuses it as\npackage.container.second_end_record, and the next step names that entry.\n\n--out names the package file. Without it, the file is <name>-<version>.metais,\nfrom the manifest, in the working directory; an --out that names an existing\ndirectory writes that file inside it. The file is written to a temporary file\n.metais-tmp-<h> beside it, created exclusively, synced, and renamed over the\nfile, so a reader never sees part of a package and a package file already there\nis replaced: replacing its own previous package is pack's edit loop, so there is\nno --overwrite. A file inside one of the layout trees, or the manifest itself,\nis refused, and so is an --out that exists and is not a regular file or a\ndirectory. --out - writes the package to standard output, and is refused when\nstandard output is a terminal; with --json the document then goes to standard\nerror, after the package.\n\n--dry-run checks, tests and builds the package as pack does, and checks where it\nwould write it, and writes nothing: it reports the package's size, digest and\nentries as pack would, and is refused exactly when pack would be refused before\nwriting. A packed package is refused as the operand: unpack it first.\n\nOperands:\n  <dir>\n      The source directory to pack; the current directory when omitted. A packed\n      package file is refused.\n\nFlags:\n  --out <file>\n      The package file to write, an existing directory to write\n      <name>-<version>.metais in, or - for standard output. Default:\n      <name>-<version>.metais in the working directory.\n  --dry-run\n      Check, test and build the package, and write nothing.\n  --limit <name>=<value>\n      Lower one resource limit for this run, to a whole number no higher than\n      its default (a size may end in KiB, MiB or GiB). metais limits lists them.\n      May be given more than once.\n  --fail-on <severity>\n      With warning, a warning fails the command with exit status 1 as an error\n      does; no finding's severity changes. Values: error, warning. Default:\n      error.\n  --json\n      Print the result as one JSON document on standard output.\n  --color <when>\n      Colour human output: auto colours a stream that is a terminal when\n      NO_COLOR is unset or empty; always and never override NO_COLOR. Values:\n      auto, always, never. Default: auto.\n  -h, --help\n      Print help to standard output and exit 0; nothing else runs.\n\nPrints:\n  The package file's name when pack chose it, its size, its package digest and\n  its entry count; with --dry-run, the entries it would pack. With --json, one\n  metais.pack/1 document.\n\nRefusals:\n  limit.invalid\n      A --limit names no limit, gives a malformed value, or tries to raise one.\n      Next: metais pack --help\n  limit.package\n      The package, or the package's bytes as pack writes them, breach one of its\n      limits; nothing was written.\n      Next: metais limits\n  output.destination.in_package\n      The package file would be inside one of the layout trees of the source\n      directory, or be its manifest.\n      Next: metais pack <dir> --out <path>\n  output.destination.not_directory\n      A directory above the package file exists and is not a directory.\n      Next: metais pack <dir> --out <path>\n  output.destination.nothing_written\n      The package file could not be written, and nothing was: its directory does\n      not exist, what stands at its name is not a regular file, a temporary file\n      .metais-tmp-<h> is left beside it, or a write failed.\n      Next: metais pack <dir>\n  package.container.second_end_record\n      An entry of the package pack built puts the end of central directory\n      signature in the package's final 65,557 bytes, so a reader refuses the\n      package; nothing was written.\n      Next: metais pack <dir>\n  usage.archive_to_terminal\n      --out - is given, and standard output is a